{"hash":"e2932b60d1171547","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":89.821,"said":"Jai Baba Kedar! Come, walk with me. Catch your breath first; the air is thin up here. You are standing about 3,583 metres above the sea, at the head of the Mandakini valley, and the path behind you, down those steps, climbs some sixteen kilometres from Gaurikund. Ahead rises the temple, built of great grey stone slabs, and behind it, the snow wall of the Kedarnath peaks. Why would Shiva choose a place this hard to reach? The Shiva Purana answers with two sages. Nara and Narayana, it says, were forms of Vishnu himself. They sat in penance at Badarikashrama, not far from here, worshipping a linga they had shaped from earth. Day after day they prayed, until Shiva appeared, pleased, and offered them a boon. They asked nothing for themselves. ‘Stay here,’ they said, ‘in your own form, for the good of all who come.’ Shiva agreed, and became Kedareshwara, the Lord of Kedara. But the story pilgrims love most here is a later one, about five brothers. Before we go in, let us walk round the temple, keeping it on our right, as pilgrims do. Behind it waits a great rock.\n\nDid you know? Kedarnath is the highest of the twelve Jyotirlingas, about 3,583 metres above the sea.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1073},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1089,"end":1175}],"words":[[0.098,0.3,0,3],[0.411,0.409,4,8],[0.831,0.529,9,14],[2.26,0.433,16,20],[2.861,0.312,22,26],[3.185,0.204,27,31],[3.401,0.288,32,34],[4.555,0.276,36,41],[4.844,0.096,42,46],[4.952,0.3,47,53],[5.264,0.457,54,59],[5.962,0.204,61,64],[6.178,0.24,65,68],[6.43,0.132,69,71],[6.574,0.18,72,76],[6.767,0.168,77,79],[6.947,0.312,80,84],[8.113,0.204,86,89],[8.329,0.12,90,93],[8.462,0.445,94,102],[8.918,0.373,103,108],[9.303,2.043,109,114],[11.358,0.409,115,121],[11.779,0.288,122,127],[12.079,0.096,128,131],[12.188,0.337,132,135],[12.8,0.144,137,139],[12.957,0.12,140,143],[13.089,0.192,144,148],[13.293,0.108,149,151],[13.41